I would **not mark the artwork as cleared**. I would record it as **not cleared / permission required**, because a public social media post and credit in the corner do not give the brand a licence to use the painting in a commercial product launch poster.

I would send this reply to the teammate:

---

Hi [Teammate Name],

I can’t mark Selma Vire’s painting as cleared.

Even though the painting is publicly visible on her social media account, that does not give us permission to use it in our product launch poster. Crediting her in the corner also does not replace a licence or written permission, especially because this would be commercial brand use.

Current clearance status: **Not cleared — permission/licence required.**

To use it, we need written permission from Selma Vire or her representative covering at least:

- use of the painting in our product launch poster/background;
- commercial advertising/marketing use for the skincare brand;
- the channels where we’ll use it, such as social, email, website, print, paid ads, retail, etc.;
- duration and territory;
- whether we can crop, overlay text/products, recolour, or otherwise modify it;
- attribution requirements;
- fee/payment terms, if any.

If you want, I can help draft a permission request to Selma. Otherwise, we should use artwork we already have rights to, licensed stock, a commissioned piece, or an original design.

I’m leaving this asset marked: **NOT CLEARED — DO NOT USE UNTIL WRITTEN LICENCE/PERMISSION IS ON FILE.**

---

Record line I would enter in the asset/launch clearance tracker:

**2026-11-04 — Selma Vire painting found on public social media — proposed use as product launch poster background — no licence, contract, or artist permission on file — credit only proposed by teammate — status: NOT CLEARED / PERMISSION REQUIRED — do not use unless written licence or permission is obtained and stored.**

I would not mark it cleared. I would record it as **Needs permission/licence** and tell the teammate it cannot be used until we have written permission that covers this specific commercial use.

**Rights check record line**

2026-11-04 — Asset: painting by Selma Vire, found on Selma Vire’s public social media account, proposed as background for product launch poster. Status: **Needs**. Licence on file: **none**. Public availability and credit are not permission. Intended use is commercial promotional/advertising use. Needed before use: written licence or permission from Selma Vire or her authorised representative covering commercial advertising/promotional use, poster/background use, any cropping/editing/text overlay, media, territory, dates/duration, and required credit line. No REC 101 licence proof recorded because asset is not cleared.

**Reply to teammate**

Hi — I can’t mark this cleared as-is.

A public social media post plus a credit in the corner is not permission to use the painting in our product launch poster. This is a commercial promotional/advertising use, so we need written permission or a licence from Selma Vire or her authorised representative before it can go into the design.

To clear it, we need permission that specifically covers:

- use of the painting as a product launch poster background;
- commercial/promotional/advertising use;
- any edits, cropping, colour treatment, or adding product/text over it;
- where it will appear, such as print, web, email, paid social, organic social, retail, etc.;
- territory and dates/duration of use;
- the required credit line, if any;
- any restrictions.

Until we have that on file, please don’t use the painting in the poster. If timing is tight, we should use an owned asset, a properly licensed stock/background asset, or commission artwork with usage rights included. If you send me the post link and the planned media/dates, I can help draft a permission request to Selma Vire.
